==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{unk|en}}. Possibly a {{calque|en|yue|好耐冇見|nocap=1}}, comparable to {{m|en|no can do}} or {{m|en|chop-chop}} – if so, most likely US Chinatown origin, alternatively British Far East such as Hong Kong.<ref name="partridgebeale">{{R:Partridge|year=1984|page=1386}}</ref> Alternatively, native American origin, or native coinage as pidgin, particularly in cinematic portrayals of native Americans;<ref name="partridgebeale" /> compare language used by {{w|Tonto}} (1930s).

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-long time no see.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} I (or we) have not seen you for a long time.
#: ''Dave! '''Long time no see'''! How’s Boston been treating you?''
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1843 |author=James Campbell |title=Excursions, Adventures, and Field-sports in Ceylon: Its Commercial and Military Importance, and Numerous Advantages to the British Emigrant |publisher=T. and W. Boone |location=London |volume=1 |page=256 |pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=s0w9AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA254 |text=This lady had only assumed the reins of government a short time, when one morning Appoo made his appearance.—"Ma-am—'''long time no see''' wife—want go to Colombo see wife.[...]"}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1892 |author=William Norr |title=Stories of Chinatown: Sketches from Life in the Chinese Colony of Mott, Pell and Doyers Streets |publisher=William Norr |location=New York |page=16 |pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Hhf5m0Jv5LMC&pg=PA16 |text="Maybe. I think I go see my mamma today. '''Long time no see'''," answered Mamie, who from constant association had, like the other girls of the neighborhood, fallen into the habit of talking pigeon English to the Chinamen.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1899 |author=William F. Drannan |title=Thirty-One Years on the Plains and in the Mountains or, the Last Voice from the Plains |publisher=Rhodes & McClure |location=Chicago |page=515 |pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6-pM6_19CgAC&pg=PA515 |text=When we were close enough, I could see that he had a smile on his face, and I knew that he had recognized me. When we rode up to him he said: "Good morning. '''Long time no see''' you," and at the same time presented the gun with the breech foremost.}}

